From c6369b93bc68f931e782ed45ad65d75dbefbfa74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duck-brain <duck-brain@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:12:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] duck-brain P16: torque-on sets acceleration 0 and
 checks it by the internal goal (67)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A correction to P11 from the stand of 17.10.2026: "41 = 1, then 41 = 0
under torque" left some servos reading 41 = 0 while their profile kept
an acceleration of 1 — 150 ms before they moved, 6 of 14 starting
60-95 ms late.

Now, after torque (40 = 1) and speed (46 = 10000), on every torque-on:
41 = 0 (read back), wait 20 ms, move the goal 3 counts off the present
position and read the internal goal (67: two bytes, low first, like 42 —
checked on the stand's 14 servos) for 10 ms — it must equal the new goal
at once. A servo whose 67 lags gets 41 = 0 again, up to 5 times; one that
still lags is logged as an error and reported in robot.health
(`feetech.accel_stuck`), torque stays on. The goal then goes back to the
present position. 41 = 1 is never written, and `robotctl servo write`
refuses a nonzero 41.

Torque comes on for the 14 joints the policy drives first, then for the
mouth on its own (the policy has no action for it); if the mouth fails,
the 14 come off again, as before. Each group logs its time and the
41 = 0 rewrites per id ("torque-on done", group="joints" / "mouth"),
and robot.health carries them: `feetech.torque_on_ms` (the 14),
`feetech.mouth_torque_on_ms`, `feetech.servos[].accel_rewrites`.
`robotctl servo torque all on` does the same and prints both.

The emulator models the stuck acceleration (Servo::stuck_accel).
